<Doc title="Orange Pi 4 Pro" subtitle="the companion board - Allwinner A733, and its 40-pin header" category="Boards" id="orangePi4Pro">

<Include file="pinouts/orangepi-4-pro.bdoc"/>

<Note><Bold>Where it sits.</Bold> This is the compute domain of docs/conventions.md -
the board that runs <Code>firmware/pilot</Code>: the lidar driver, the link to
the Pico, and the reactive controller. It is powered by its own supply, never the
ESC's BEC, and it meets the car domain at exactly one wire: common ground. The
Pico and the RPLIDAR C1 hang off its <Bold>USB</Bold> ports, not this header; the
header is for what comes next - the sensored motor's hall lines, a status lamp, a
stop button.</Note>

<Warn><Bold>Every GPIO on this header is 3.3 V.</Bold> The manual is explicit
(section 3.15: 28 GPIO ports, all at 3.3 V). The Pico's pins are 3.3 V too, so the
two boards can talk over GPIO directly - but nothing from the car domain may land
here: the 6 V BEC, the ESC's 7.2 V pack, and the 5 V hall harness of the motor all
need a divider or a level shifter first. Pins 2 and 4 are the USB-C input rail,
5 V; they are a supply, not a signal.</Warn>

<Table>
<Row head="yes"><Cell>bus</Cell><Cell>header pins</Cell><Cell>overlay</Cell></Row>
<Row><Cell class="serial">I2C0</Cell><Cell>3 SDA, 5 SCL</Cell><Cell>i2c0</Cell></Row>
<Row><Cell class="serial">I2C1</Cell><Cell>27 SDA, 28 SCL</Cell><Cell>i2c1</Cell></Row>
<Row><Cell class="serial">I2C2</Cell><Cell>19 SDA, 23 SCL</Cell><Cell>i2c2</Cell></Row>
<Row><Cell class="serial">I2C3</Cell><Cell>26 SDA, 21 SCL</Cell><Cell>i2c3</Cell></Row>
<Row><Cell class="serial">SPI3</Cell><Cell>19 MOSI, 21 MISO, 23 CLK, 24 CS0</Cell><Cell>spi3</Cell></Row>
<Row><Cell class="serial">UART7</Cell><Cell>8 TX, 10 RX</Cell><Cell>uart7</Cell></Row>
<Row><Cell class="digital">PWM</Cell><Cell>7, 29, 32, 33, 35, 36, 37, 38</Cell><Cell>pwm0..7, spwm2</Cell></Row>
</Table>

<Note>I2C2 and I2C3 borrow SPI3's pins (19, 23 and 21), so SPI3 and those two
buses cannot be on at once. Once enabled they appear as <Code>/dev/i2c-0</Code>
to <Code>/dev/i2c-3</Code>, <Code>/dev/spidev*</Code>, a <Code>/dev/ttyS*</Code>
and <Code>/sys/class/pwm/pwmchip0</Code> (pin 7 alone is on
<Code>pwmchip20</Code>).</Note>

<Warn><Bold>Every bus above is OFF until its overlay is enabled.</Bold> Out of the
box the header is 28 plain GPIOs; <Code>sudo orangepi-config</Code>, System,
Hardware, tick the overlay, reboot. A device wired to pins 3 and 5 with
<Code>i2c0</Code> still off answers <Code>i2cdetect</Code> with nothing, which
reads exactly like a dead sensor. As shipped on this board only the internal
buses show up (<Code>/dev/i2c-5, 7, 8, 9, 13, 15, 20</Code> and
<Code>/dev/ttyS0, S1, S6</Code>); none of those is on the header.</Warn>

<Table>
<Row head="yes"><Cell>naming</Cell><Cell>pin 7 is</Cell><Cell>used by</Cell></Row>
<Row><Cell>physical</Cell><Cell>7</Cell><Cell>silkscreen, this page, the manual</Cell></Row>
<Row><Cell>wiringOP</Cell><Cell>wPi 2</Cell><Cell><Code>gpio write 2 1</Code></Cell></Row>
<Row><Cell>sysfs</Cell><Cell>gpio356</Cell><Cell><Code>/sys/class/gpio</Code>, libgpiod</Cell></Row>
<Row><Cell>SoC port</Cell><Cell>PWM0-2 (manual: PL4)</Cell><Cell>the A733 datasheet, overlays</Cell></Row>
</Table>

<Note><Bold>Four numbering schemes, and they disagree.</Bold> The diagram carries
the physical number on the outside, the wiringOP name in colour, and
<Code>gpioNNN wPi N</Code> in the white cell - because a pin you can name three
ways is a pin you can wire wrong three ways. <Code>gpio readall</Code> on the
board prints the same table live, with the current level of every pin; it is the
first thing to run when a signal does nothing.</Note>

<Note><Bold>Sources.</Bold> The numbers on the diagram are <Code>gpio readall</Code>
from wiringOP 2.46 on this board (bibobox), 2026-09-07, regenerated by
<Code>tools/opi4pro_pinout.py</Code>. The bus tables are the Orange Pi 4 Pro user
manual v1.4 (orangepi.net, OrangePi_4_Pro_A733_User-Manual_v1.4.pdf), sections
3.15 and 3.17 - the SPI, I2C, UART and PWM tests. The board on the bench:
Ubuntu Jammy "Orange Pi 1.0.6", kernel 5.15 sun60iw2, aarch64, 8 cores,
5.7 GiB, NVMe.</Note>

<Note><Bold>What the car will want from here.</Bold> The sensored 21.5T motor's
hall harness is three digital lines - the encoder odom.hxx has been waiting for
- and after a level shift they belong on three plain GPIOs (PL9, PL8, PL12 on
pins 11, 12, 13 are free and adjacent). An emergency-stop button belongs on one
more, pulled up, with the pilot treating a low as STOP the same way SPACE does in
the hub. Nothing else on the car needs this header.</Note>

</Doc>
